      exercises: [
        { type: 'mc', q: 'Which sentence contains a dangling participle?', options: ['Having reviewed the contract, the lawyer recommended two amendments.', 'Walking into the office, the report was lying on the desk.', 'Audited by an independent firm, the accounts were accepted.', 'Once approved, the budget cannot be altered.'], answer: 1, explain: 'The report cannot walk; the implied subject of "walking" must be the subject of the main clause.' },
        { type: 'fill', q: 'Reduce the relative clause: "The documents that were submitted late will not be considered." → "The documents ___ late will not be considered."', answer: ['submitted'], explain: 'A passive relative clause (that were submitted) reduces to the past participle.' },
        { type: 'fill', q: 'Nominalize: "The company failed to pay, so it was fined." → "The company\'s ___ to pay resulted in a fine."', answer: ['failure'], explain: 'The verb "fail" becomes the noun "failure"; the clause becomes a noun phrase.' },
        { type: 'tf', q: '"The report which I wrote" can be reduced to "The report writing".', answer: false, explain: 'Reduction is only possible when the relative pronoun is the subject of its clause; here "which" is the object.' },
        { type: 'order', q: 'Put the words in order to form a sentence with a perfect participle clause.', items: ['Not having received', 'a reply', 'within ten days,', 'we', 'sent', 'a reminder.'], explain: 'Negative perfect participle: Not + having + past participle, then the main clause with the same subject.' },
        { type: 'match', q: 'Match the informal expression to its formal equivalent.', pairs: [['look into', 'investigate'], ['put off', 'postpone'], ['find out', 'ascertain'], ['before', 'prior to'], ['then', 'subsequently']], explain: 'Formal register prefers Latinate single verbs and adverbs to phrasal verbs and everyday words.' },
        { type: 'listen', text: 'Having underestimated demand, the firm ran out of stock within the first week.', q: 'Listen and write the sentence.', explain: 'Perfect participle "having underestimated" shows the earlier action; "ran out of" is a three-part phrasal verb.' },
        { type: 'mc', q: 'Which option shows the best sentence variety for a formal report?', options: ['The costs rose. The revenue fell. The company borrowed. The board worried.', 'Costs rose while revenue fell; faced with this gap, the company borrowed heavily, a decision that alarmed the board.', 'The costs rose and the revenue fell and the company borrowed and the board worried.', 'Rising the costs, falling the revenue, borrowing the company, the board worried.'], answer: 1, explain: 'The correct option mixes a compound sentence, a participle opening and an appositive; the others are repetitive or ungrammatical.' },
        { type: 'write', q: 'Rewrite the following informal note as a formal report paragraph (100–140 words), using at least two participle clauses, two nominalizations and no phrasal verbs or contractions: "We looked into the expenses process. People put off submitting receipts, so we can\'t close the month on time. Also, nobody checks claims under 200, which isn\'t great. We think we should set up an online form and get managers to approve stuff weekly."', model: 'Having examined the expenses process, the review team identified two principal weaknesses. First, the late submission of receipts by staff prevents the timely closure of monthly accounts. Second, claims below 200 are not subject to any verification, leaving a significant proportion of expenditure without oversight. Both weaknesses were confirmed by the finance team. Taken together, these weaknesses increase both the risk of error and the workload at month-end. It is therefore recommended that an online submission form be introduced and that managers approve claims on a weekly basis. Implemented together, these measures would reduce processing time and improve the accuracy of the monthly figures.', checklist: ['At least two participle clauses (Having examined…, Implemented together…)?', 'At least two nominalizations (submission, closure, verification, introduction)?', 'No phrasal verbs (look into, put off, set up) and no contractions?', 'Impersonal structures (It is recommended that…)?', 'Each participle clause has the same subject as its main clause?', 'Between 100 and 140 words?'] },
      ],
